      <tei:div type="colophon" subtype="introduction">
        <tei:p>Shnei Zeitim, by Shlomo ibn Gabirol, was once the most beloved song for Ḥanukkah. Though in recent years it has largely been replaced in popular consciousness by Maoz Tsur, in rites which preserve the customs of piyyut recitation within the Shema service it is still a beloved part of the holiday. The piyyut, a <tei:hi rend="italic">meorah</tei:hi> — intended to introduce the ḥatimah for the Yotser Or blessing before the Shema, is included here with an original translation. Interestingly enough, Shnei Zeitim has an anti-Hasmonean focus, with emphasis on the importance of separation of powers between priest and king. For further discusson of this aspect, I’d recommend Yitzhak Szyf’s article on The Lehrhaus.</tei:p>
      </tei:div>
      <j:endConditional target="#osp_59422_introduction"/>
